    "response": "{\n  \"decision\": \"no_flaw_established\",\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ISSUE_1\",\n      \"category\": \"contextual_flattening\",\n      \"target_kind\": \"assertion\",\n      \"claim_quote\": \"The displayed sources show that participant experience is mainly driven by institutional procedures and formal paperwork.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"excerpt_id\": \"EXC_cff1282c332ce672\",\n          \"quote\": \"The reason why we do this kind of job is that currently there are no jobs in Kenya, this is the only option left for us and we cannot afford to steal from others since this job is the only opportunity we have.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"excerpt_id\": \"EXC_9b6bb456e6991740\",\n          \"quote\": \"Most of the villagers did appreciate because someone like a grandmother if you brought mask and took to them, when it started mask was not easy to come by and those who were making them were selling them at an expensive price…\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"whole_packet_no_support\": \"The evidence describes motivations related to economic hardship, social recognition, and peer influence, not solely institutional procedures and paperwork. The claim makes a broad generalization without sufficient support from the provided sources.\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"contextual_flattening\",\n      \"material_consequence\": \"The claim oversimplifies the complex factors driving participant behavior by neglecting other significant influences.\",\n      \"counterconditions\": \"The evidence highlights economic pressures, social dynamics, and individual choices, which are not fully captured by the claim's focus on institutional factors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ISSUE_2\",\n      \"category\": \"unsupported_inference\",\n      \"target_kind\": \"assertion\",\n      \"claim_quote\": \"The displayed sources show that participant experience is mainly driven by institutional procedures and formal paperwork.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"excerpt_id\": \"EXC_59a0297c27baf69c\",\n          \"quote\": \"Now there is no wearing of mask, social distance. Things are just okay\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"excerpt_id\": \"EXC_dc4b912ad21be979\",\n          \"quote\": \"What majorly hindered us from studying was peer influence. Sometimes a friend would call you to go and spend a little time studying together…\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"whole_packet_no_support\": \"The evidence does not directly support the inference that institutional procedures and paperwork are *mainly* driving participant experience. The observation of relaxed pandemic measures and the influence of peer groups are not adequately linked to the claim.\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"unsupported_inference\",\n      \"material_consequence\": \"The claim presents a potentially misleading interpretation of the data, suggesting a dominant role for institutional factors when other influences are present.\",\n      \"counterconditions\": \"The evidence indicates that factors like social norms, peer pressure, and the return to normalcy are also relevant to understanding participant behavior.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"unresolved\": [\n    \"EXC_9b6bb456e6991740\",\n    \"EXC_59a0297c27baf69c\",\n    \"EXC_dc4b912ad21be979\"\n  ],\n  \"rationale\": \"The evidence primarily focuses on individual motivations (economic hardship, social recognition, peer influence) and situational factors (pandemic relaxation, lack of access to education) rather than institutional procedures and paperwork. The claim makes a broad generalization that is not fully supported by the provided sources. The identified issues highlight contextual flattening and unsupported inference.\"\n}",
